### Cluster S02-C6 — the policy modal's behaviour: the scroll gate and the a11y wiring

Edits `apps/ui/components/consent/PrivacyPolicyModal.tsx`; creates
`tests/render/consent-policy-modal-behaviour.test.tsx`. **Follows C5** (same component file).

**The criterion, from R15, evaluated on the scroll container and not restated per step:**
`container.scrollTop + container.clientHeight >= container.scrollHeight - 8`, evaluated (a) at
mount, (b) on every `scroll` of that container, (c) on `resize`; once true it **latches**.
**The 8px slack is derived:** the body text is 11.5px at `line-height: 1.65` ≈ 19px per line,
so 8px is under half a line — the marker cannot count as reached while a line of policy is
still hidden — while still absorbing sub-pixel and zoom rounding, which exact equality does not.

**How a jsdom test drives it, measured by ARCH-S02** (`scratchpad/arch-consent-s02/env-probe.mjs`):
`Object.defineProperty(el, "scrollTop"|"clientHeight"|"scrollHeight", { value, configurable: true, writable: true })`
shadows jsdom's prototype getters (which all return `0`) and **can be redefined between
assertions** — `configurable: true` is load-bearing, without it the latch case cannot scroll
back up.

- **S02-S40 · The boundary: `scrollHeight - 9` is not reached, `scrollHeight - 8` is.**
  Serves R15. Creates the test file; edits the component.
  **Test:** define `clientHeight: 200`, `scrollHeight: 500`; set `scrollTop: 291`, dispatch
  `scroll` → assert `I have read it` is `disabled`; set `scrollTop: 292`, dispatch `scroll` →
  assert it is not.
  **Property:** the comparison is `>= scrollHeight - 8`, not `>`, `>=  scrollHeight`, or a
  different slack. **Derivation of 291/292:** `291 + 200 = 491 < 492` and `292 + 200 = 492 >= 500 - 8`.
  **RED before, GREEN after. Acceptance:** `Tests 2 passed (2)`. Cluster **C6**.

- **S02-S41 · The gate latches: scrolling back to the top leaves it enabled.**
  Serves R15. Edits both files.
  **Test:** reach the end, then redefine `scrollTop: 0` and dispatch `scroll`. **Property:**
  the button is still not `disabled`. **RED before, GREEN after. Acceptance:** `Tests 3 passed (3)`.
  Cluster **C6**.

- **S02-S42 · A policy short enough to need no scrolling enables the button at mount.**
  Serves R15. Edits both files.
  **Test:** define `scrollHeight === clientHeight === 200` before the effect runs and dispatch
  no event. **Property:** the criterion is satisfied at mount and no special case is written.
  **RED before, GREEN after. Acceptance:** `Tests 4 passed (4)`. Cluster **C6**.

- **S02-S43 · The criterion is re-evaluated on `resize`.**
  Serves R15. Edits both files.
  **Test:** mount with metrics that do not satisfy it; redefine them so they do; dispatch
  `resize` on `window` (not on the container). **Property:** the button enables without any
  `scroll` event. **RED before, GREEN after. Acceptance:** `Tests 5 passed (5)`. Cluster **C6**.

- **S02-S44 · While disabled the button is announced as disabled and says why; no tooltip.**
  Serves R15. Edits both files.
  **Test:** before the gate opens, the button has `disabled`, `aria-disabled === "true"`, and
  an accessible description resolving to `Scroll to the end of the policy to continue.`
  (an `aria-describedby` at an element carrying exactly that text); and the button has **no**
  `title` attribute.
  **Property:** the reason is available to a screen reader and the SPEC's "no tooltip is added"
  holds. **RED before, GREEN after. Acceptance:** `Tests 6 passed (6)`. Cluster **C6**.

- **S02-S45 · The scroll region is keyboard-reachable and named.**
  Serves R16. Edits both files.
  **Test:** the scroll container has `tabindex === "0"` and
  `getAttribute("aria-label") === "Privacy Policy text"` — **the string is pinned here, in the
  plan, exactly as `S02-S44` pins its own** (`ARCH-REV-S02-r1` N4: the previous wording asserted
  equality "against the label the component sets", which makes the expected value whatever the
  implementation chose, so that half of the assertion could not fail and only the `tabindex`
  half discriminated).
  **Derivation of the string:** the dialog's own accessible name is already its title
  (`What we store, and why`, `S02-S34`), so the region needs a name that says what is *inside*
