## Clusters (id · steps · ONE verification command · file surface · mutant class)

**Authored by ARCH-S02, 2026-09-06. Every command below was RUN by its author at authoring
time (charge A9); the classification and the verbatim summary lines are in §A9.**

### The ONE verification idiom, written out verbatim because it is the thing that was executed

`TOOLING-TRAPS.md` records six variants of "an acceptance command that looks like verification
and verifies nothing". **ARCH-S02 measured a seventh in this lane and it is why the third arm
exists:** `pnpm exec vitest run <existing-file> <file-that-does-not-exist>` runs only the
existing file, prints `Tests 17 passed (17)` and **exits 0** — a cluster command naming a file
that was never created reports GREEN having never executed it. The remedy is an anchored
`Test Files <n> passed (<n>)` arm with `<n>` the exact number of paths in the command.

This is the exact function ARCH-S02 executed against all nine clusters
(`scratchpad/arch-consent-s02/a9.sh`); `$1` is `<n>`, the rest are the file paths:

```sh
run() {
  id="$1"; n="$2"; shift 2
  out=$(pnpm exec vitest run "$@" 2>&1); vt=$?
  sum=$(printf '%s' "$out" | grep -E '^[[:space:]]*Tests[[:space:]]+' | tail -1)
  fil=$(printf '%s' "$out" | grep -E '^[[:space:]]*Test Files[[:space:]]+' | tail -1)
  printf '%s' "$sum" | grep -qE "^[[:space:]]*Tests[[:space:]]+[1-9][0-9]* passed" \
    && ! printf '%s' "$sum" | grep -q 'failed' \
    && printf '%s' "$fil" | grep -qE "^[[:space:]]*Test Files[[:space:]]+${n} passed \(${n}\)"; guard=$?
  [ "$vt" -eq 0 ] && [ "$guard" -eq 0 ]; v=$?
  echo "$id | vt=$vt guard=$guard VERDICT=$v"
}
```

**Four load-bearing properties, each closing a measured variant:** capture first, so nothing
can steal the runner's exit status (variant 3) · **anchor** to the summary line, so a test's
TITLE cannot satisfy the guard (variant 4) · require a **nonzero** pass count and **reject a
summary containing `failed`** (variant 2) · assert the **file count**, so a filter that matched
nothing cannot pass (variant 7, measured here).

**Every term above is pure ASCII, deliberately** (`COMMON.md` §10.16). `Tests`, `Test Files`,
`passed` and `failed` are ASCII words on the summary lines. **No term in this plan matches
vitest's `✓`/`×` status glyph**, because in a `.sh` file under `/bin/bash` the shell gets BSD
`grep` in the C locale, where `.` matches ONE BYTE and those glyphs are 2-3 bytes — a term that
matched the glyph would be `0` forever in any script, in CI, and in the three-run loop, while
matching happily in this tool's `ugrep`-shim shell. Where a failing test must be NAMED (the G2
delta below), the anchor is the **test name**, never the glyph.

**`run_c9` — the C9 variant, adding the two merge arms** (`ARCH-REV-S02-r1` **N1**: the round-1
C9 command claimed a mutant it could not detect. Nine vitest files contained exactly one that
reads `globals.css`, and its seven steps assert only about S02's OWN block, so losing S01's
block failed nothing):

```sh
run_c9() {
  n="$1"; shift
  run S02-C9 "$n" "$@"; rc=$?
  s01=$(grep -c -- '--scrim:' apps/ui/app/globals.css)          # S01's two token blocks
  s02=$(grep -c -- '=== consent-ui S02 ===' apps/ui/app/globals.css)   # S02's open + end markers
  echo "S02-C9 | mergeArms: --scrim:=$s01 (expect 2)  S02markers=$s02 (expect 2)"
  [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$s01" -eq 2 ] && [ "$s02" -eq 2 ]
}
```

**Why `2` and `2`, derived not conventional:** `--scrim` is declared once in each of the two
token blocks S01 owns (`:root` and `html[data-mode="chamber"]`), so `2` is "both of S01's
blocks survived the merge"; S02's block is delimited by an opening and a closing marker
(`S02-S59`), so `2` is "S02's block survived, exactly once". **This makes C9 unable to go green
before the merge, and that is correct, not a defect:** `S02-S66` — the merge — is C9's FIRST
step. At base both arms read `0`, which is one more declared reason C9 is RED there (§A9).

**THE CHAIN RULE, new in rework round 1** (`ARCH-REV-S02-r1` **N7** — mandatory, and it is the
structural gap B1 fell through). **Every cluster that edits `apps/ui/components/SignUpFlow.tsx`
runs the test file of every EARLIER cluster in that chain, with `<n>` raised accordingly.** The
chain is `C3 → C4 → C7`, and `tests/unit/v2ui-node-runner.test.ts` is in all three because it
is the source guard that scans that same component (`COMMON.md` §10.18: a cluster that edits a
file a scanning test covers RUNS that test in its own command):

| Cluster | files, in command order | `<n>` |
|---|---|---|
| C3 | `consent-signup-group` · `auth-flow-integration` · `v2ui-node-runner` | 3 |
| C4 | `consent-signup-gate` · **`consent-signup-group`** · `auth-flow-integration` · `v2ui-node-runner` | **4** (was 3) |
| C7 | `consent-signup-modal` · **`consent-signup-group`** · **`consent-signup-gate`** · `auth-flow-integration` · **`v2ui-node-runner`** | **5** (was 2) |
| C9 | all eight S02 files · `auth-flow-integration` · **`v2ui-node-runner`** | **10** (was 9) |

**The class has a SECOND chain, and the finding sampled only the first.** `heartbeat-protocol`
§2.2: a reported finding is a sample of a class, never the whole class. N7 names `C4` and `C7`;
the same shape is in `C5 → C6`, which also share a component file
(`apps/ui/components/consent/PrivacyPolicyModal.tsx`, §Concurrency). `C6`'s round-1 command
listed only `consent-policy-modal-behaviour.test.tsx`, so `C5`'s
`consent-policy-modal-render.test.tsx` was never re-run by `C6` and a `C6` regression into
`C5`'s render pins — a dropped section, a broken `aria-labelledby`, a `Download PDF` control
reintroduced — was invisible until `C9`. **`C6`'s command therefore becomes 2 files.** The
sweep over every cluster pair sharing a product file is complete and is the table above plus
this paragraph: `C1`/`C2`/`C8` share a product file with nobody, so they are unaffected.

**The known-GOOD / known-BAD validation and the per-term mutant table are in §A9 (parts 2, 3
and 4) and are NOT restated here** — two formulations of one rule is the B4 defect class, and
§A9 is where they were executed. Round 1's four-case validation, which ARCH-REV-S02 reproduced,
is subsumed by that table: A/B there are parts 2A/2B here, C is the **T3 variant-7** mutant, D
is the **T2** mutant.

**Run every command from the level holding `package.json`** —
`.worktrees/consent-s02/dialectical-engine/`, not the worktree root, where every command
returns `No test files found` (`TOOLING-TRAPS.md`, "The worktree root is NOT the project root").

**The three-run law:** each cluster's ONE command runs three times and the WORST run is the
verdict. Green-green-red is RED.

### The cluster map

