### Concurrency — which clusters may run at the same time, and which must follow

Two clusters may run concurrently **iff they share no file**. Measured over the surfaces above:

- **`S02-C1` ∥ `S02-C2`** — disjoint (`modalSemantics.ts` vs `privacyPolicy.ts`; different test
  files). **These two are the mission's opening move** and neither waits on the other lane.
- **`S02-C8` ∥ everything** — `globals.css` and its own test are in no other cluster's surface.
  It is nevertheless **scheduled second-to-last**, because its selectors must match the class
  names `S02-S17`, `S02-S34` and `S02-S37` introduce. Concurrency is available if a second seat
  is free and the class names are agreed from this PLAN.
- **`S02-C3` → `S02-C4` → `S02-C7`** — strictly sequential; all three write
  `apps/ui/components/SignUpFlow.tsx`.
- **`S02-C5` → `S02-C6`** — strictly sequential; both write `PrivacyPolicyModal.tsx`.
- **`S02-C5` requires `S02-C1` and `S02-C2`** (it imports both modules).
- **`S02-C7` requires `S02-C3`, `S02-C4`, `S02-C5`, `S02-C6`.**
- **`S02-C9` requires all of them**, and is the only cluster that touches the other lane.

**Also concurrent, and it is the point of the cut: `S02-C3`/`S02-C4` (the `SignUpFlow` chain)
runs in parallel with `S02-C5`/`S02-C6` (the modal chain)** — they share no file. A two-seat
schedule is C1∥C2 → {C3→C4} ∥ {C5→C6} → C7 → C8 → C9.

### Standing gates — run and REPORTED by every cluster, deliberately NOT folded into the guard

`BASELINE.md`'s rule is "assert the DELTA, never the absolute". Two of the three gates below
are **RED at base by design**, so folding them into the cluster guard — which asserts a GREEN
summary with no `failed` — would make every cluster command permanently red and unable to
discriminate anything. They are therefore separate, named, and classified under §A9 beside the
cluster commands.

| Gate | Command (from the lane's `dialectical-engine/`) | The DELTA that is the acceptance |
|---|---|---|
| **G1 typecheck** | `pnpm run generate:contract && pnpm typecheck` | **MONOTONE DELTA, three asserted arms:** `generate:contract` exit `0` · `grep -cE '^\$ tsc --noEmit$'` on the captured output is `1` (**the run actually happened** — without this the guard passes vacuously when the script does not run, `COMMON.md` §10.19) · **zero** diagnostics outside `tests/unit/s14-ui.test.ts`. The pinned total (`8`, measured) is **reported, never asserted** — it is another mission's number and this guard must not go red the day they repair it (`TOOLING-TRAPS.md`). A seat reports "no diagnostic outside the pin", **never "green"**. `generate:contract` must run first — `packages/contract/generated/**` is gitignored and absent from a fresh worktree. |
| **G2 token contract** | `pnpm exec vitest run tests/unit/t9-mode-tokens.test.ts` | **NAMED-FAILURE-SET DELTA (`COMMON.md` §10.12) + HIT-LIST COUNT (§10.18), anchored on ASCII test NAMES and never on the `×` glyph:** exit `1` · exactly **2** lines matching `^ FAIL +tests/unit/t9-mode-tokens\.test\.ts > ` · one of them ending `> renders one accessible toggle that reads the document mode, flips it, and persists it` and one ending `> leaves no mode-inert colour literal in the four Wave-0 product files` · the colour-literal **hit list counted at exactly 1** (`^\+ +"/.*:[0-9]+:`) with the pinned element (`.../apps/ui/app/globals.css:<n>:background: color-mix(...)`) asserted **separately so it cannot silently vanish** (the line number may shift). A third failing test, a different failing test, or a second hit is S02's finding. |
| **G3 live source guard** | `pnpm exec vitest run tests/unit/v2ui-node-runner.test.ts` | exit 0, `Tests 2 passed (2)`. This is the guard that actually bites for R21 — `tests/architecture/auth-front-door-parity.test.ts` carries the same `terms` ban at `:86` but is RED at base on two `ENOENT`s and never reaches its assertions. |

`tests/architecture/auth-front-door-parity.test.ts` is **RED at base and inherited, never
claimed**: exit 1, `Tests 2 failed (2)`, both `ENOENT` on `web/package.json` and
`web/components/LoginFlow.tsx`. S02 does not touch it and must not report it as anything else.

### A9 — every cluster command RUN at base and PROVEN SATISFIABLE (rework round 1)

**Re-run in full by ARCH-S02-REWORK-R1 under `COMMON.md` §10.16-§10.20**, because N7 changed
three of the nine commands (`C4` 3→4 files, `C7` 2→5, `C9` 9→10), the chain-rule sweep changed
a fourth (`C6` 1→2), and N1 added the two merge arms to `C9`. Round 1's A9 measured a command
set that no longer exists.

Run in `.worktrees/consent-s02/dialectical-engine` at `2b670d30`, branch `slice/consent-s02`,
**lane `git status --porcelain` = 0 entries before and after**. Classified by CAUSE
(`COMMON.md` §10.17): **BROKEN** = the command itself cannot run
(`startup error|unexpected argument|failed to load|usage:|command not found`, or
`no test files found` for a file that EXISTS); **RED-by-design** = `No test files found` /
`cannot find module` / `Failed to resolve import` for a file or module this PLAN creates;
**GREEN** = the summary line. The three cause counters are printed separately
(`brokenSigs`, `cannotFindModule`, `noTestFiles`) so no reader has to infer which one fired.

**A9 is a satisfiability proof, not a "does it run" check.** The proof has four parts and all
four were executed: (1) every command run at base; (2) the guard fed **known-GOOD** synthetic
captures, returning `VERDICT=0`; (3) **one mutant per guard term**, each flipping it; (4) the
whole thing run **twice** — once inline in the authoring shell and once **from a `.sh` file
under `/bin/bash`** — with both verdicts reported. The script is
`scratchpad/arch-s02-rework-r1/a9.sh`. **Packet note: this seat's `allowed` list names new
probe files as `arch-s02-rework-r1-*.mjs`, and §10.16 mandates a `.sh`; the script therefore
lives in scratch, which the same list allows, and the orchestrator copies it at seat exit
(`COMMON.md` §10.11).**

**Why both shells, and why every guard term in this plan is ASCII.** Measured:

```
grep flavour, INLINE in the authoring shell : ugrep 7.8.4 aarch64-apple-macosx +neon/AArch64
grep flavour, INSIDE the .sh under /bin/bash: grep (BSD grep, GNU compatible) 2.6.0-FreeBSD
LC_ALL inside the script                     : C
```

Two different `grep` implementations under two different locales. **The two verdicts agree
line for line**, because no term in this plan matches vitest's `✓`/`×` status glyph — those are
2-3 bytes and `.` matches ONE BYTE in the C locale, so a glyph-matching term is `0` forever in
any script, in CI and in the three-run loop while matching happily inline. Where a failing test
must be identified, the anchor is the **test NAME** (`^ FAIL +<file> > <suite> > <name>$`), which
is what gate **G2** below does.

