| **S02-C9** | nothing — **no product edit**; the merge is the orchestrator's | every file |

### The single-writer rule against S01, and the mechanics of the cross-slice dependency

**The only file both slices write is `apps/ui/app/globals.css`.** S01 appends
`/* === consent-ui S01 === */ … */` at the end; **S02 appends
`/* === consent-ui S02 === */ … /* === end consent-ui S02 === */` after it and touches nothing
above its own opening marker** (`S02-S59` asserts both halves). The two blocks conflict
trivially at merge time; that is **accepted** (vertical-slice law §6) and the resolution is
both blocks in that order. It is not a reason to serialize the lanes.

**Two S02-owned TypeScript files are consumed unchanged by S01:**
`apps/ui/components/consent/modalSemantics.ts` (S01's card takes its focus trap, focus return
and the Esc stack from it — S01-R18) and `apps/ui/components/consent/PrivacyPolicyModal.tsx`
(S01's `Privacy notice` link opens it with `mode="read"` — S01-R20, whose interface paragraph
is byte-identical to S02-R14 and which **neither slice may change alone**).

**The dependency measured in both directions, and why it is NOT a deadlock:**

- **S02 needs four of S01's tokens** — `--ok-edge`, `--scrim`, `--z-policy-scrim`,
  `--z-policy-card` (measured: `grep -c -- '<token>:' apps/ui/app/globals.css` → `0` for each;
  the fifth token `SPEC.md` R22 names, `--ok-dot`, **already exists** with `2` declarations and
  is not S01's to add).
- **But no S02 cluster is blocked by that.** `modalSemantics.ts` and `privacyPolicy.ts`
  reference no token; the two components reference tokens only through CSS classes; and
  `S02-C8`'s CSS may be written and fully verified against an undeclared custom property,
  because `tests/unit/t9-mode-tokens.test.ts:376-377` asserts set equality over
  **declarations** and its colour-literal gate (`:541-561`) scans for `#hex`/`rgb(`/`oklch(`
  literals — a `var()` reference is neither. **Only the browser appearance waits on S01.**
- **The merge is therefore ONE step and it is LAST:** `S02-S66` in cluster `S02-C9`.
  **The orchestrator performs `git merge slice/consent-s01` inside the lane; a coding seat
  never runs `merge`.** The seat records the commit it received —
  `git log -1 --format=%h slice/consent-s01` — and the merge commit, in its handoff.
- **In the other direction:** S01's lane pulls `slice/consent-s02` after `S02-C1` is committed
  (the helper), and again after `S02-C5`/`S02-C6` (the modal). That is S01's PLAN's business;
  S02's obligation is only to land `S02-C1` **first**, which is why it is the first cluster.

## Refutation table (step · failure caught · failure NOT caught)

**Authored by ARCH-S02.** Every step owes an honest second column. Where jsdom cannot observe
the thing, the second column says so and names the V step that can.

| Step | Failure this step's test CATCHES | Failure it does NOT catch |
|---|---|---|
| S02-S01 | Two surfaces closing on one `Escape`; a per-surface listener; a FIFO instead of a LIFO stack | Whether a real browser delivers the key to `document` at all (V step 7); an `Escape` typed while focus is outside the modal |
| S02-S02 | Initial focus left on `body`, or landing on the first focusable rather than the named one | Whether the focus ring is *visible* in either mode (V steps 1-14, `--focus`) |
| S02-S03 | Focus dropped to `body` on close | Focus return when the opener has been removed from the DOM meanwhile |
| S02-S04 | A trap that lets Tab escape forward to the page behind | Browser-native focus order differences (jsdom does not implement sequential focus navigation; the test drives the handler) |
| S02-S05 | A trap implemented in one direction only — the single commonest way to half-build one | As S02-S04 |
| S02-S06 | A focusable list cached at open, so `I have read it` is unreachable after the gate opens | A control made focusable by CSS (`visibility`, `display`) rather than by attribute |
| S02-S07 | A scrim handler that closes on any click inside the modal | Pointer-down-inside/up-outside drags, which a real browser reports as a scrim click |
| S02-S08 | An unguarded `window.matchMedia` — **which throws in every render test that mounts the modal** (measured: `undefined` in jsdom 30.0.1) | Whether the OS/browser setting is actually honoured (V, with reduced motion enabled) |
| S02-S09 | A stack that never pops, swallowing every later `Escape` | A surface unmounted without closing (React `StrictMode` double-invocation is not exercised) |
| S02-S10 | A second `document` keydown listener — the exact defect REQ-REV-01 B3 predicts two parallel lanes would produce | A listener added on `window` instead of `document` |
| S02-S11 | Pills sorted into document order — the coder's reflex the design forbids | A label with a typo that is still in the right position **(caught by the deep equality, not by the sort check)** |
| S02-S12 | A dropped, duplicated or renumbered section | A body string swapped between two sections **(caught by S02-S16)** |
| S02-S13 | Bullets added to a section that has none, or a list truncated | A bullet with the right count and the wrong text **(caught by S02-S16)** |
| S02-S14 | A pill pointing at a section that does not exist, or at the wrong one | Whether clicking it scrolls **(V step 12; the CALL is pinned by S02-S48)** |
| S02-S15 | An accent taken from document order rather than R12's table (e.g. section 09 given `--con`) | Whether the token renders the design's colour (V steps 11, and the token values are S01's) |
| S02-S16 | A `\uXXXX` escape shipped as six visible characters; any body or bullet mistranscription | A character that is visually identical but a different codepoint in **both** the SPEC and the module |
| S02-S17 | The group missing, or one input outside it | Whether the box *looks* like the artboard (V steps 1-2) |
| S02-S18 | The old 18+ wording surviving; a comma or capital wrong in either sentence | Whether `Privacy Policy` is visibly underlined and bold (V step 1) |
| S02-S19 | `required` dropped from either tag; a `<div role="checkbox">` substituted; a second `<form>` | Native constraint validation, which a bare `new Event("submit")` bypasses — so `required` gates nothing in any test here (`auth-flow-integration.test.tsx:41-48`) |
| S02-S20 | An input with no accessible name, or one naming the wrong row | Whether a screen reader announces the link inside the name usefully |
| S02-S21 | The 18+ row opening the modal; a row whose text does not toggle its input | Pointer/touch behaviour on the 17px hit target (V steps 1-3) |
| S02-S22 | The `privacy-accepted` input being absent or misnamed (`field()` fails on a null lookup); **a line inserted into the wrong case, or into only two of the three — the `3`/`3` count arm, which the pass count cannot see** (N6) | Nothing about the button — **this is an assignment and assignments never move the button** |
| S02-S23 | A fifth argument added to `client.register`; a reordered argument list | A changed value inside one of the four (the case pins all four literally) |
| S02-S24 | A class name, comment or attribute containing `terms`/`privacy notice`/`localStorage` entering `SignUpFlow.tsx` | The same substrings in `PrivacyPolicyModal.tsx` or `privacyPolicy.ts`, which the guard does not read — and which is exactly why the policy prose lives there |
| S02-S25 | A button left `disabled={busy \|\| sent}` — i.e. the gate never implemented | An implementation that hard-codes `disabled` to `true` **(that is S02-S28's job)** |
| S02-S26 | A gate written as `a \|\| p` instead of `a && p` | As S02-S25 |
| S02-S27 | The same, from the other side; a mirror wired to only one input | As S02-S25 |
| S02-S28 | `disabled` hard-coded `true` — the mutant no other case in the slice's gate suite catches, now caught in **C7** · an acknowledgement that closes the modal without ticking the box, or ticks the box without setting the R17 mirror (the paired assertion `checked === true` **and** `disabled === false`) · **a regression to the bare-click route, which after C7 can only pass if a bare click ticks the box — the thing V's goal forbids** | Whether the button *looks* enabled (V step 8) · any route that ticks the box outside the acknowledgement — those are `S02-S49`…`S02-S51`, `S02-S54`…`S02-S56`, `S02-S71`. **It is no longer a GREEN-before-and-after control:** in C7 the modal does not exist until this cluster wires it, so the case fails first (B3) |
