| S02-S72 | The ADR absent, or written `Accepted` when only V may accept it, or naming a surface the module does not export (the four export-name arms) | **Whether the ADR's reasoning is RIGHT — an ADR is a record, and V ratifies it; the arms check that the record exists and matches the code's surface, never that the decision was wise** |

**Which steps are the idiom rule below** (stated by REQ-01 in the next two paragraphs and NOT
restated here — two formulations of one rule is the B4 defect class): the button-state steps
that use `field(name).click()` inside `act` are `S02-S25`…`S02-S30` and the second half of
`S02-S53`; the handler/`FormData` steps that correctly use `field(x).checked = true` are
`S02-S22`, `S02-S31`, `S02-S32` and the deliberate no-dispatch halves of `S02-S29` and
`S02-S30`. Every step touching a checkbox says which of the two it is, in its own text.

Every step owes an honest second column. The SPEC already concedes where jsdom cannot observe
the thing and V must: the checkbox group's and modal's pixel geometry (S02-R01, S02-R09), the
jump pills' actual scrolling (S02-R11 — jsdom pins the mapping, V pins the scroll), and the
narrow-viewport behaviour (V step 13). Those belong in the right-hand column, not in a test
that pretends to cover them. Note also that a bare `new Event("submit")` bypasses HTML
constraint validation (`tests/render/auth-flow-integration.test.tsx:41-48`), so `required`
alone catches nothing in a test — S02-R18's handler-level refusal is what the pin must
exercise.

**Two failure modes this slice's steps must not pretend to catch (REQ-REV-01 B2, narrowed by
B4/B5, measured):**
(a) `field(x).checked = true` fires no React `change`, so it can never move the submit
button's `disabled` state — a step whose test asserts the BUTTON after an assignment is
asserting nothing and will read as an implementation bug when it fails. **Button-state steps use
exactly one idiom: `field(name).click()` inside `await act(async () => { … })`.** Not a
`change` dispatch: `dispatchEvent(new Event("change", { bubbles: true }))` and `dispatchEvent(new
Event("click", { bubbles: true }))` are **measured dead** — React routes a checkbox's `onChange`
through the click event and requires a real `MouseEvent`, so neither even changes the DOM box
(`.hermes/reports/consent-ui/probes/b2-idiom-matrix.mjs`, three identical runs). And `.click()`
**toggles**, so a step that pre-assigns `.checked = true` must set it back to `false` before
clicking — see `SPEC.md` R17 cases 5 and 6, which state the mechanism and say which case catches
which regression. (b) Conversely, a step that exercises R18's
refusal is testing the HANDLER and `FormData`, so the assignment idiom is correct there and a
dispatched change is unnecessary. Every step touching the checkbox state states which of the
two it is.
